:"http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/","license":"CC BY 4.0","material":"preprint"},{"url":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/","license":"CC-BY-4.0","imposing":"Springer"}],"texkeys":["KAGRA:2013rdx","KAGRA:2020npa","Abbott:2020qfu","KAGRA:2013pob","Aasi:2013wya"],"citeable":true,"imprints":[{"date":"2020-09-28"},{"date":"2018-04-26"}],"keywords":[{"value":"Gravitational waves","source":"author"},{"value":"Gravitational-wave detectors","source":"author"},{"value":"Electromagnetic counterparts","source":"author"},{"value":"Data analysis","source":"author"},{"value":"black hole: binary","schema":"INSPIRE"},{"value":"neutron star: binary","schema":"INSPIRE"},{"value":"black hole: mass","schema":"INSPIRE"},{"value":"detector: network","schema":"INSPIRE"},{"value":"gravitational radiation","schema":"INSPIRE"},{"value":"localization","schema":"INSPIRE"},{"value":"VIRGO","schema":"INSPIRE"},{"value":"LIGO","schema":"INSPIRE"},{"value":"KAGRA","schema":"INSPIRE"},{"value":"gravitational radiation detector","schema":"INSPIRE"},{"value":"detector: sensitivity","schema":"INSPIRE"},{"value":"electromagnetic field: production","schema":"INSPIRE"},{"value":"gravitational radiation: emission","schema":"INSPIRE"},{"value":"binary: coalescence","schema":"INSPIRE"},{"value":"binary: compact","schema":"INSPIRE"},{"value":"data analysis method","schema":"INSPIRE"},{"value":"network","schema":"INSPIRE"},{"value":"observatory","schema":"INSPIRE"},{"value":"capture","schema":"INSPIRE"}],"refereed":true,"abstracts":[{"value":"We present our current best estimate of the plausible observing scenarios for the Advanced LIGO, Advanced Virgo and KAGRA gravitational-wave detectors over the next several years, with the intention of providing information to facilitate planning for multi-messenger astronomy with gravitational waves. We estimate the sensitivity of the network to transient gravitational-wave signals for the third (O3), fourth (O4) and fifth observing (O5) runs, including the planned upgrades of the Advanced LIGO and Advanced Virgo detectors. We study the capability of the network to determine the sky location of the source for gravitational-wave signals from the inspiral of binary systems of compact objects, that is BNS, NSBH, and BBH systems. The ability to localize the sources is given as a sky-area probability, luminosity distance, and comoving volume. The median sky localization area (90\\% credible region) is expected to be a few hundreds of square degrees for all types of binary systems during O3 with the Advanced LIGO and Virgo (HLV) network. The median sky localization area will improve to a few tens of square degrees during O4 with the Advanced LIGO, Virgo, and KAGRA (HLVK) network. We evaluate sensitivity and localization expectations for unmodeled signal searches, including the search for intermediate mass black hole binary mergers.","source":"arXiv"},{"value":"We present our current best estimate of the plausible observing scenarios for the Advanced LIGO, Advanced Virgo and KAGRA gravitational-wave detectors over the next several years, with the intention of providing information to facilitate planning for multi-messenger astronomy with gravitational waves. We estimate the sensitivity of the network to transient gravitational-wave signals for the third (O3), fourth (O4) and fifth observing (O5) runs, including the planned upgrades of the Advanced LIGO and Advanced Virgo detectors. We study the capability of the network to determine the sky location of the source for gravitational-wave signals from the inspiral of binary systems of compact objects, that is binary neutron star, neutron star–black hole, and binary black hole systems. The ability to localize the sources is given as a sky-area probability, luminosity distance, and comoving volume. The median sky localization area (90% credible region) is expected to be a few hundreds of square degrees for all types of binary systems during O3 with the Advanced LIGO and Virgo (HLV) network. The median sky localization area will improve to a few tens of square degrees during O4 with the Advanced LIGO, Virgo, and KAGRA (HLVK) network. During O3, the median localization volume (90% credible region) is expected to be on the order of $10^{5}, 10^{6}, 10^{7}\\mathrm {\\ Mpc}^3$ for binary neutron star, neutron star–black hole, and binary black hole systems, respectively. The localization volume in O4 is expected to be about a factor two smaller than in O3. We predict a detection count of $1^{+12}_{-1}$($10^{+52}_{-10}$) for binary neutron star mergers, of $0^{+19}_{-0}$($1^{+91}_{-1}$) for neutron star–black hole mergers, and $17^{+22}_{-11}$($79^{+89}_{-44}$) for binary black hole mergers in a one-calendar-year observing run of the HLV network during O3 (HLVK network during O4). We evaluate sensitivity and localization expectations for unmodeled signal searches, including the search for intermediate mass black hole binary mergers.","source":"Springer"},{"value":"We present possible observing scenarios for the Advanced LIGO, Advanced Virgo and KAGRA gravitational-wave detectors over the next decade, with the intention of providing information to the astronomy community to facilitate planning for multi-messenger astronomy with gravitational waves. We estimate the sensitivity of the network to transient gravitational-wave signals, and study the capability of the network to determine the sky location of the source. We report our findings for gravitational-wave transients, with particular focus on gravitational-wave signals from the inspiral of binary neutron star systems, which are the most promising targets for multi-messenger astronomy. The ability to localize the sources of the detected signals depends on the geographical distribution of the detectors and their relative sensitivity, and $90\\%$ credible regions can be as large as thousands of square degrees when only two sensitive detectors are operational. Determining the sky position of a significant fraction of detected signals to areas of 5– $20~\\mathrm {deg}^2$ requires at least three detectors of sensitivity within a factor of $\\sim 2$ of each other and with a broad frequency bandwidth. When all detectors, including KAGRA and the third LIGO detector in India, reach design sensitivity, a significant fraction of gravitational-wave signals will be localized to a few square degrees by gravitational-wave observations alone.","source":"Springer"}],"references":[{"record":{"$ref":"https://inspirehep.net/api/literature/1094844"},"raw_refs":[{"value":"J Aasi: The characterization of Virgo data and its impact on gravitational-wave searches, ['Class.Quant.Grav.,29,155002'], DOI: 10.1088/0264-9381/29/15/155002","schema":"text"}],"reference":{"dois":["10.1088/0264-9381/29/15/155002"]},"curated_relation":false},{"record":{"$ref":"https://inspirehep.net/api/literature/1249443"},"raw_refs":[{"value":"J Aasi: Enhancing the sensitivity of the LIGO gravitational wave detector by using squeezed states of light, ['Nature Photon.,7,613-619'], DOI: 10.1038/nphoton.2013.177","schema":"text"}],"reference":{"dois":["10.1038/nphoton.2013.177"]},"curated_relation":false},{"record":{"$ref":"https://inspirehep.net/api/literature/1227366"},"raw_refs":[{"value":"J Aasi: Parameter estimation for compact binary coalescence signals with the first generation gravitational-wave detector network, ['Phys.Rev.,D88,062001'], DOI: 10.1103/PhysRevD.88.062001","schema":"text"}],"reference":{"dois":["10.1103/PhysRevD.88.062001"]},"curated_relation":false},{"record":{"$ref":"https://inspirehep.net/api/literature/1257716"},"raw_refs":[{"value":"J Aasi: First searches for optical counterparts to gravitational-wave candidate events, ['Astrophys.J.Suppl.,211,7'], DOI: 10.1088/0067-0049/211/1/7","schema":"text"}],"reference":{"dois":["10.1088/0067-0049/211/1/7"]},"curated_relation":false},{"record":{"$ref":"https://inspirehep.net/api/literature/1294627"},"raw_refs":[{"value":"J Aasi: Method